WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.269 --> 00:00:05.799
Morality tends to get lost very quickly when
you start working at the SCP Foundation.

00:00:05.799 --> 00:00:10.260
During the whirlwind of basic training, site
orientation, and learning the difference between

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:14.830
Euclid, Keter and Thaumiel object classes,
you have to make peace with the fact that,

00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:19.340
as a new member of Foundation staff, you’ll
probably end up having to turn a blind eye

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:22.360
to doing some morally compromising things.

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:26.670
Then again, it’s such a normal, everyday
occurrence at the Foundation that almost everyone

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:32.050
working for them is desensitized to whatever
ethically dubious or downright violent tasks

00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:33.470
they have to partake in.

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:38.040
And, at the end of the day, everyone knows
that it’s all for the greater good, to keep

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:41.960
anomalies securely in containment for the
benefit and protection of humanity.

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:46.851
But the one part of Foundation life that Simon
Bennet just couldn’t reason with, was the

00:00:46.851 --> 00:00:49.380
way the very existence of D Class personnel.

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:53.960
Now, anyone with previous knowledge of the
SCP Foundation’s dubious practices will,

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:56.440
of course, be aware of D Class.

00:00:56.440 --> 00:01:00.899
But to Simon – who had just been recruited
as a member of site security personnel – the

00:01:00.899 --> 00:01:04.720
whole concept was not only alien, but completely
abhorrent.

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:08.979
D Class personnel, as he’d come to learn,
were completely expendable.

00:01:08.979 --> 00:01:13.909
Their ranks were filled with convicted felons,
almost all of them guilty of violent crimes.

00:01:13.909 --> 00:01:18.460
The Foundation seemed to have a habit of plucking
inmates from the highest security prisons

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:23.100
and penitentiaries in the world, covering
their tracks by staging the deaths of these

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:25.710
unwilling recruits into D Class.

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:29.679
From there, these criminals were essentially
used as little more than human lab rats.

00:01:29.679 --> 00:01:33.920
And it was this part that really didn’t
sit well with Simon.

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:35.999
The very idea left him torn.

00:01:35.999 --> 00:01:40.999
On the one hand, D Class were arguably the
worst of the worst; most, if not all, of them

00:01:40.999 --> 00:01:46.509
were guilty of horrific and brutal crimes,
some that could never been forgiven by anyone.

00:01:46.509 --> 00:01:51.909
But on the other, Simon struggled to rationalize
taking these prisoners from their incarceration,

00:01:51.909 --> 00:01:55.609
only to subject them to anomalous creatures
and entities.

00:01:55.609 --> 00:01:59.909
Most SCPs could do things to a D Class that
were almost as bad as what these convicts

00:01:59.909 --> 00:02:05.189
had done to their victims, and some could
dole out far, far worse fates.

00:02:05.189 --> 00:02:08.119
Did their actions make their cruel fates justifiable?

00:02:08.119 --> 00:02:11.890
Did D Class personnel deserve that amount
of punishment for their crimes?

00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:16.060
Simon didn’t know, it was a perplexing moral
conundrum.

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:19.590
Most days at the Foundation, those questions
echoed around his head.

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:24.340
He was probably better suited to a role in
the ethics committee than rank and file security.

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:28.280
He was uncertain if it was better to leave
a violent murderer to serve their sentence

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:33.800
in prison until their eventual execution,
or to abduct them and use them as a D Class

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:34.800
guinea pig.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:38.680
It wasn’t long before his fellow security
officers tried to dissuade Simon from questioning

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:43.740
the Foundation’s methods, reminding him
how lucky he was to be selected to join them,

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:49.790
and how an easy application of amnestics could
take that all away, leaving him not only jobless

00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:51.630
but his memory wiped as well.

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:55.860
Ultimately, as weeks turned into months that
then became years in the blink of an eye,

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:59.010
Security Officer Bennet’s moral compass
was ground down.

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:03.170
He started getting used to the idea of the
Foundation’s way of doing things, asking

00:03:03.170 --> 00:03:08.570
fewer and fewer questions to oppose how morally
and ethically ambiguous a lot of their experiments

00:03:08.570 --> 00:03:09.920
and procedures were.

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:15.010
Obedience to orders slowly became apathy,
and the Simon that had once been concerned

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:20.940
with the ethics of D Class experimentation
soon began looking at them as expendable assets,

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:23.380
just like the rest of the SCP Foundation did.

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:28.620
He even started to resent a few of them that
seemed to have it easy, on the few occasions

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:32.600
a member of D Class personnel got to interact
with the friendly kind of anomaly.

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:36.940
It didn’t seem fair, Simon thought, that
while he and the other security personnel

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:41.580
had to put their lives on the line whenever
a dangerous SCP breached containment.

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:45.959
Meanwhile, a D Class – who might have been
a multiple murderer or domestic terrorist

00:03:45.959 --> 00:03:52.680
– got to sit in a testing chamber with a
friendly entity like SCP-999: the Tickle Monster.

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:56.510
Keeping up to date with the Special Containment
Procedures for the various anomalous objects

00:03:56.510 --> 00:04:01.680
and beings held at the Foundation was an essential
part of a security officer’s job.

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:06.310
Ongoing testing and new discoveries that were
about an entity often led to them needing

00:04:06.310 --> 00:04:09.800
new, updated measures to keep them securely
contained.

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:15.010
But it was during a routine check of these
procedures that Simon came across something…

00:04:15.010 --> 00:04:16.010
strange.

00:04:16.010 --> 00:04:21.150
Well, maybe not so strange when you work at
the SCP Foundation, but definitely a noticeable

00:04:21.150 --> 00:04:23.199
level of strangeness.

00:04:23.199 --> 00:04:27.129
Reading through the Containment Procedures
for one of the anomalies housed at the site,

00:04:27.129 --> 00:04:31.419
Simon found a list of instructions, fairly
standard stuff for the Foundation.

00:04:31.419 --> 00:04:35.449
The strange part was that he was sure that
he had never read this list before.

00:04:35.449 --> 00:04:42.310
But at the same time, it wasn’t a new update
recently added to an entry in the SCP Archive.

00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:47.830
It was paradoxically brand new information,
a detailed description of containment measures

00:04:47.830 --> 00:04:53.919
yet also, seemingly, something that had been
written and archived ages ago.

00:04:53.919 --> 00:04:58.240
Reading through the instructions, they described
a process that was essential to keeping one

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:00.319
of the site’s anomalies secure.

00:05:00.319 --> 00:05:04.889
It had even been given its own name, the Special
Containment Procedures referring to the whole

00:05:04.889 --> 00:05:07.919
practice as a ‘Monthly Termination’.

00:05:07.919 --> 00:05:13.460
The more that Simon read, his eyes would widen
further, as he learned exactly what steps

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:15.110
this process entailed.

00:05:15.110 --> 00:05:19.500
In his earlier days he would’ve thought
of this so-called Monthly Termination as excessive,

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:22.879
a needlessly cruel act even by the standards
of the SCP Foundation.

00:05:22.879 --> 00:05:28.509
But that was the old Simon, a man who hadn’t
yet become jaded by his work, before he had

00:05:28.509 --> 00:05:33.889
come to adopt the same apathy and disregard
for life as his fellow Foundation personnel.

00:05:33.889 --> 00:05:38.100
Reaching the end of the passage describing
Monthly Termination, Simon alerted the other

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:42.129
security officers, making sure they read the
procedure too.

00:05:42.129 --> 00:05:48.259
Some unknown feeling stirred deep within Simon,
telling him this needed to be carried out,

00:05:48.259 --> 00:05:51.479
and it had to happen right now.

00:05:51.479 --> 00:05:55.419
Before long, more members of staff had been
told to read the entry on Monthly Termination,

00:05:55.419 --> 00:05:58.070
each one passing it on to their colleagues.

00:05:58.070 --> 00:06:02.710
Then, Simon and a large detachment of other
guards began marching towards the cell blocks

00:06:02.710 --> 00:06:05.330
where D Class personnel were imprisoned.

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:08.370
Almost a third of the site’s incarcerated
inmates were rounded up.

00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:10.879
It couldn’t have been any more than a few
hundred.

00:06:10.879 --> 00:06:14.539
Simon wasn’t sure, he couldn’t see all
of them from where he was standing.

00:06:14.539 --> 00:06:16.379
But mostly, he didn’t care.

00:06:16.379 --> 00:06:20.979
All he could focus on was following the Monthly
Termination procedure to the letter.

00:06:20.979 --> 00:06:25.310
Together, he and the security detachment escorted
the collected D Class personnel through the

00:06:25.310 --> 00:06:30.669
facility, single-file, all marching in efficient
and perfectly synchronized motion.

00:06:30.669 --> 00:06:33.120
It was almost like a choreographed military
display.

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:38.430
If he hadn’t been so preoccupied with thinking
only of the procedure, Simon might have realized

00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:40.659
how strange all this was.

00:06:40.659 --> 00:06:44.639
All the personnel were moving at once, in
such flawless timing.

00:06:44.639 --> 00:06:46.409
Even though this hadn’t been planned in
advance.

00:06:46.409 --> 00:06:50.580
In fact, he and the other personnel had only
just learned about Monthly Termination.

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:55.779
And yet, they were carrying it out as if they
had done it a thousand times before.

00:06:55.779 --> 00:06:59.839
The security officers continued ferrying the
D Classes through the facility, eventually

00:06:59.839 --> 00:07:02.289
leading the convicted felons to the main entrance.

00:07:02.289 --> 00:07:06.759
The doors were opened without question, nobody
who had read the Monthly Termination instructions

00:07:06.759 --> 00:07:09.449
saw what was going on as anything out of the
ordinary.

00:07:09.449 --> 00:07:15.499
A long, winding row of inmates in Foundation-issue
orange jumpsuits was then guided out of the

00:07:15.499 --> 00:07:20.240
site’s main doors, instructed by the security
to keep walking even further.

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:22.220
None of them knew how far they’d really
walked.

00:07:22.220 --> 00:07:26.090
A lot of the D Class personnel complained
and moaned, but the few that tried to leave

00:07:26.090 --> 00:07:30.689
the single-file line were quickly put back
in place by the security detachment.

00:07:30.689 --> 00:07:34.969
Most of them were directly verbally aggressive,
cursing and threatening the security officers.

00:07:34.969 --> 00:07:39.129
But they still seemed compliant, walking the
way they were told to.

00:07:39.129 --> 00:07:43.650
It was as if they had to obey, almost like
their legs wouldn’t let them resist.

00:07:43.650 --> 00:07:47.969
One begged to be let go, turning to Simon
and pleading not to be taken wherever it was

00:07:47.969 --> 00:07:49.120
they were going.

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:53.779
“I’m sorry,” Simon replied plainly,
with barely any emotion behind his words.

00:07:53.779 --> 00:07:55.839
It was a lie; he didn’t feel sorry.

00:07:55.839 --> 00:07:59.119
In fact, he hadn’t even been sure why he
apologized at all.

00:07:59.119 --> 00:08:04.259
The reply had happened almost unnaturally,
like something else was speaking through him.

00:08:04.259 --> 00:08:07.119
He wasn’t even sure how far away they’d
gotten from the facility.

00:08:07.119 --> 00:08:10.559
It might have felt like miles if he had been
thinking about it.

00:08:10.559 --> 00:08:13.039
But he wasn’t, none of the officers were.

00:08:13.039 --> 00:08:17.240
They were following their instructions, totally
unaware that they’d been walking for so

00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:19.399
long that they were starting to get blisters.

00:08:19.399 --> 00:08:22.080
They all somehow knew they had gotten far
enough.

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:25.909
Beyond the reach and sight of the Foundation
facility they’d started in.

00:08:25.909 --> 00:08:30.110
So far, in fact, that the surveillance systems
wouldn’t be able to see them.

00:08:30.110 --> 00:08:34.640
Still moving with the same, completely synchronous
timing, Simon and the other security officers

00:08:34.640 --> 00:08:37.970
herded the D Class personnel towards a wooded
clearing.

00:08:37.970 --> 00:08:41.550
They formed a ring around the inmates, before
pushing them down onto their knees.

00:08:41.550 --> 00:08:45.320
A few of them hung their heads, some even
shedding a tear.

00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:47.960
They had already figured out what was about
to happen.

00:08:47.960 --> 00:08:51.470
Those that hadn’t quickly caught up when
they saw the security officers covering or

00:08:51.470 --> 00:08:55.450
deactivating their head cameras… then reaching
for their sidearms.

00:08:55.450 --> 00:08:59.040
When they returned to the facility, Simon
and the other officers would all give the

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:00.100
same story.

00:09:00.100 --> 00:09:04.760
They collectively led a third of the site’s
D Class to an area out of surveillance range,

00:09:04.760 --> 00:09:06.600
and executed them all with their handguns.

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:10.190
At least, that’s how they all remembered
the incident.

00:09:10.190 --> 00:09:14.970
But what they thought, they’d done didn’t
quite match what had actually happened.

00:09:14.970 --> 00:09:18.871
Naturally, the Foundation higher-ups quickly
got wind of what had happened, and of such

00:09:18.871 --> 00:09:22.870
a massive expenditure of their expendable
D Class resources.

00:09:22.870 --> 00:09:27.080
The incident forced the SCP Foundation to
launch a full-blown investigation into the

00:09:27.080 --> 00:09:31.690
actions of Simon, the other security officers,
and additional personnel on-site.

00:09:31.690 --> 00:09:35.880
They quickly recognized the pattern of behavior;
they had seen it before.

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:39.840
They already knew not one of the guards had
actually fired their weapons.

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:44.050
In fact, none of them had been acting of their
own free will.

00:09:44.050 --> 00:09:48.370
Simon and the others had no idea that the
Monthly Termination procedure didn’t exist

00:09:48.370 --> 00:09:55.290
in the Foundation’s records, and was actually
the symptom of an SCP-2193 event.

00:09:55.290 --> 00:09:58.890
All those D Class that had been rounded up
and marched out into the middle of nowhere

00:09:58.890 --> 00:10:03.560
were still gone, though; not shot point-blank
like the officers thought, but the prisoners

00:10:03.560 --> 00:10:06.020
were still no longer alive either.

00:10:06.020 --> 00:10:10.600
Although he’d never remember it, when Simon
had been stood amongst his fellow officers,

00:10:10.600 --> 00:10:13.430
one of the D Class had begun floating upwards.

00:10:13.430 --> 00:10:18.180
Lifted off the ground by some invisible force,
the convicted felon drifted about three meters

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:19.250
in the air.

00:10:19.250 --> 00:10:23.550
Without warning, the prisoner was then launched
up, practically fired towards the sky like

00:10:23.550 --> 00:10:28.240
a rocket, traveling into the upper atmosphere
at several times the speed of sound.

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:32.680
One by one, the members of D Class all began
experiencing the same thing, floating off

00:10:32.680 --> 00:10:37.610
the ground only to be catapulted up to the
stratosphere with such speed and force that

00:10:37.610 --> 00:10:39.960
they vanished from view in seconds.

00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:44.020
Meanwhile, all Simon and the other security
officers did was stand by and watch, keeping

00:10:44.020 --> 00:10:46.670
their head cams covered or switched off.

00:10:46.670 --> 00:10:50.560
The last of the D Class prisoners stood in
the clearing, the one that had asked Simon

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:51.660
to be set free.

00:10:51.660 --> 00:10:56.550
He was still asking, begging to be helped
down as his feet were hoisted up against his

00:10:56.550 --> 00:11:00.930
will, practically screaming for someone, anyone,
to do something.

00:11:00.930 --> 00:11:05.370
His eyes locked with Simon’s for a split
second, before he too was sent hurtling into

00:11:05.370 --> 00:11:06.540
the sky.

00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:11.120
Moving that high and that fast isn’t something
a human being is at all likely to survive.

00:11:11.120 --> 00:11:15.890
Now rid of the D Class that they had led to
their deaths, all the gathered security personnel

00:11:15.890 --> 00:11:16.890
looked up.

00:11:16.890 --> 00:11:21.090
It was night, starlight cast across an inky
canvas of dark sky.

00:11:21.090 --> 00:11:24.880
But Simon was fixated on the moon, they all
were.

00:11:24.880 --> 00:11:29.810
None of them could take their eyes off it
as it slowly appeared to blink, like a watchful

00:11:29.810 --> 00:11:32.420
eye hanging in space above them.

00:11:32.420 --> 00:11:36.470
Not one of them would remember what they had
seen, or what they had truly done.

00:11:36.470 --> 00:11:39.550
No member of D Class was a saint, it’s true.

00:11:39.550 --> 00:11:43.790
They were murderers, criminals, and dangerous
individuals.

00:11:43.790 --> 00:11:48.460
Maybe some of them were so bad that they did
deserve to be used as test subjects, to die

00:11:48.460 --> 00:11:50.120
at the hands of anomalous entities.

00:11:50.120 --> 00:11:54.340
But then again, does anyone really deserve
to go out like that?

00:11:54.340 --> 00:11:56.650
Little is known about the point of origin
of SCP-2193.

00:11:56.650 --> 00:12:02.000
While it affects the documents stored within
the Foundation’s database, it does not function

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:05.230
like a computer virus or artificial intelligence.

00:12:05.230 --> 00:12:11.850
Instead, SCP-2193-1 is an anomalous piece
of information, or an ‘infohazard’ as

00:12:11.850 --> 00:12:13.510
SCPs like this are known.

00:12:13.510 --> 00:12:17.870
It has been known to infect digital files,
specifically the Special Containment Procedures

00:12:17.870 --> 00:12:23.970
for various entities, objects, and creatures
that the Foundation has recorded in its archives.

00:12:23.970 --> 00:12:27.770
Even their most talented computer engineers
and anomalous researchers cannot determine

00:12:27.770 --> 00:12:31.030
how this infohazard is able to accomplish
this.

00:12:31.030 --> 00:12:35.920
It seems to infect files at random, without
any regular pattern in its timing or the type

00:12:35.920 --> 00:12:38.090
of SCP files it appears in.

00:12:38.090 --> 00:12:45.220
Upon infection, a file now containing SCP-2193-1
will include references to a Monthly Termination

00:12:45.220 --> 00:12:51.500
of D Class personnel, marking it as an essential
process for keeping an anomaly secure.

00:12:51.500 --> 00:12:55.990
Anyone that reads this suffers from what is
known as a memetic effect; essentially, they

00:12:55.990 --> 00:13:01.250
will believe what they have read and believe
that they have always been aware of this information.

00:13:01.250 --> 00:13:08.090
On top of that, a person infected by SCP-2193-1
will accept it as true that the Monthly Termination

00:13:08.090 --> 00:13:11.760
is a legitimate and accepted practice within
the SCP Foundation.

00:13:11.760 --> 00:13:16.860
They can’t be convinced that killing hundreds
of D Class personnel is anything less than

00:13:16.860 --> 00:13:17.860
justified.

00:13:17.860 --> 00:13:22.230
Now, you might be forgiven for thinking that
uninfected personnel can easily explain to

00:13:22.230 --> 00:13:27.950
those who have been affected by SCP-2193-1
that the Monthly Termination isn’t an official

00:13:27.950 --> 00:13:29.090
Foundation policy.

00:13:29.090 --> 00:13:34.260
But this infohazard has proven itself to be
particularly infectious when spreading its

00:13:34.260 --> 00:13:36.000
memetic influence.

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:42.020
Any member of Foundation personnel not yet
exposed to SCP-2193-1 can be easily convinced

00:13:42.020 --> 00:13:47.220
that the Monthly Termination is both morally
acceptable and a Foundation-held procedure

00:13:47.220 --> 00:13:50.850
that is absolutely necessary for anomaly containment.

00:13:50.850 --> 00:13:54.750
If anyone that had read – and therefore
believes – the Monthly Termination process,

00:13:54.750 --> 00:13:59.300
then they will display a lack of cognitive
dissonance whenever somebody attempts to present

00:13:59.300 --> 00:14:02.630
them with information that conflicts with
SCP-2193-1.

00:14:02.630 --> 00:14:09.130
While no document within their internal database
has a record of the Monthly Termination procedure,

00:14:09.130 --> 00:14:14.530
back in January of 1999, the Foundation was
able to witness the process secondhand via

00:14:14.530 --> 00:14:16.190
one of its agents.

00:14:16.190 --> 00:14:21.810
During an SCP-2193 event, Agent Ysson’s
head camera was left uncovered, recording

00:14:21.810 --> 00:14:27.200
excerpts of the precise and perfectly choreographed
execution of numerous D Class personnel.

00:14:27.200 --> 00:14:32.010
Much like the event Simon was a part of, an
unknown anomaly caused the Earth’s moon

00:14:32.010 --> 00:14:33.850
to appear to blink.

00:14:33.850 --> 00:14:38.070
It is believed, by some within the Foundation,
that this is the result of a form of eldritch

00:14:38.070 --> 00:14:43.040
entity, a being that demands a substantial
human sacrifice every month.

00:14:43.040 --> 00:14:45.980
This being, as of yet, remains unidentified.

00:14:45.980 --> 00:14:50.740
Just as unclear is this anomaly’s intentions,
motives, or how it is able to affect – or

00:14:50.740 --> 00:14:53.030
perhaps use – the moon.

00:14:53.030 --> 00:14:56.810
Of course, the SCP Foundation has its own
personnel stationed up there.

00:14:56.810 --> 00:15:03.190
However, during recorded instances of SCP-2193
events, their agents at Luna Area 32 have

00:15:03.190 --> 00:15:08.200
reported no unusual activity or abnormalities
at the corresponding times.

00:15:08.200 --> 00:15:12.970
The running theory among some personnel is
that the moon isn’t this unidentified being’s

00:15:12.970 --> 00:15:17.920
actual eye, but some kind of ocular device
like a spyglass that allows it to observe

00:15:17.920 --> 00:15:19.820
the Monthly Termination.

00:15:19.820 --> 00:15:24.120
Whatever this being is, it apparently possesses
an immense level of power.

00:15:24.120 --> 00:15:30.010
How exactly it is able to create SCP-2193-1
and infiltrate the Foundation’s internal

00:15:30.010 --> 00:15:33.730
database with that infohazard is still unknown.

00:15:33.730 --> 00:15:39.990
As is the exact reason why it goes to such
trouble, such incalculable lengths, seemingly

00:15:39.990 --> 00:15:43.640
just for the purpose of killing so many D
Class inmates.

00:15:43.640 --> 00:15:48.500
The immediate assumption – albeit admittedly
a morbid and cynical one – held by most

00:15:48.500 --> 00:15:54.160
Founation researchers studying SCP-2193 is
that this entity needs this almost ritualistic

00:15:54.160 --> 00:15:57.640
monthly sacrifice in order to sustain itself.

00:15:57.640 --> 00:16:02.400
One idea suggests it could be a creature that
opposes how the Foundation uses its D Class

00:16:02.400 --> 00:16:03.600
personnel.

00:16:03.600 --> 00:16:08.300
Maybe it, just like Simon used to, questions
the morality of convicts being used as expendable

00:16:08.300 --> 00:16:09.490
guinea pigs.

00:16:09.490 --> 00:16:13.850
And as such, it uses its eldritch levels of
power to remove them from the Foundation’s

00:16:13.850 --> 00:16:14.880
reach for good.

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:20.870
Or perhaps, despite its subterfuge, this being
feeds on those who have wronged others, making

00:16:20.870 --> 00:16:23.440
D Class personnel prime candidates.

00:16:23.440 --> 00:16:28.080
That would certainly explain why the Monthly
Termination procedure causes security officers

00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:31.910
to only bring the Foundation’s force of
expendable convicts.

00:16:31.910 --> 00:16:37.110
And after all, there are benevolent SCPs out
there, even friendly ones, so it stands to

00:16:37.110 --> 00:16:41.250
reason that this being could be trying to
rid the world of people who are bad enough

00:16:41.250 --> 00:16:42.510
to be put into D Class.

00:16:42.510 --> 00:16:46.800
But then again, this world is rarely so kind.

00:16:46.800 --> 00:16:52.760
SCP-2193-1 makes Foundation officers bring
D Classes out to where they can’t be seen,

00:16:52.760 --> 00:16:58.170
causes them to cover their head cameras and
believe a phony story about execution by sidearm

00:16:58.170 --> 00:17:00.780
so nobody knows what really happens to them.

00:17:00.780 --> 00:17:05.570
It even hides the infoharzard detailing the
Monthly Termination process at random, moving

00:17:05.570 --> 00:17:09.060
it around to a new file whenever it's detected.

00:17:09.060 --> 00:17:13.230
Such great effort is taken by this entity
to cover its tracks, which would suggest a

00:17:13.230 --> 00:17:15.260
far more nefarious goal.

00:17:15.260 --> 00:17:20.420
It’s entirely possible that this being does
all this simply to amuse itself, expending

00:17:20.420 --> 00:17:26.420
countless lives and Foundation resources purely
for a sick, sadistic form of entertainment.

00:17:26.420 --> 00:17:32.150
With no way of knowing the entity’s true
intention, the SCP Foundation can only sit

00:17:32.150 --> 00:17:37.760
and vigilantly wait, an artificial intelligence
surveying their database for the next instance

00:17:37.760 --> 00:17:39.309
of SCP-2193.

00:17:39.309 --> 00:17:48.140
Now go check out “SCP-055 ANTI-MEME/UNKNOWN”
and “SCP-1025 ENCYCLOPEDIA OF COMMON DISEASES”

00:17:48.140 --> 00:17:54.400
for more insights into infohazards, scare-inducing
secrets, and truths too terrifying to tell…

00:17:54.400 --> 00:17:55.750
but only if you’re brave enough!

